<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wat Phra Mahathat Woramahawihan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wat Phra Mahathat Woramahawihan is the chief Buddhist temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-si-thammarat">Nakhon Si Thammarat</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. Its great white stupa, the Phra Borommathat Chedi, built in Sri Lankan style, is believed to enshrine relics of the Buddha and became one of the most revered pilgrimage sites in the region. The temple reflects the city's role as a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a> from the 13th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wat-phra-mahathat-woramahawihan.jpg" alt="Wat Phra Mahathat Woramahawihan" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Suwannee.payne · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%98%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B4%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
